<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arizona</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Arizona is a state in the south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, with its capital at Phoenix. Home to the Hohokam, Ancestral Puebloan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/navajo">Navajo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apache">Apache</a> and other peoples, it was a frontier of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> until most of it passed to the United States in 1848, with the south added by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gadsden-purchase">Gadsden Purchase</a> of 1853. It became a territory in 1863 and the 48th state in 1912, and contains the Grand Canyon.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arizona.jpg" alt="Arizona" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original: Unknown author Vector: Madden · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Arizona.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Arizona lies in the south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and is one of the Four Corners states, bordering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colorado">Colorado</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-mexico">New Mexico</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utah">Utah</a>, as well as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nevada">Nevada</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>, with an international border with the Mexican states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sonora">Sonora</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baja-california">Baja California</a> to the south. It covers about 295,000 square kilometres and is the sixth-largest state by area. The south is a desert Basin and Range region with extremely hot summers and mild winters, rich in cactus and other drought-adapted plants.</p><p>The north-central high country belongs to the Colorado Plateau, with mountains, mesas, deep canyons and forests of pine, Douglas fir and spruce. The Mogollon Rim, an escarpment of about 609 metres, marks the plateau's south-western edge. Despite its dryness, 27 per cent of the state is forest, and it holds the world's largest stand of ponderosa pine.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>For thousands of years before the modern era Arizona was home to Native American civilizations, among them the Hohokam, Mogollon and Ancestral Puebloan cultures, whose pueblos, cliff dwellings and rock paintings survive. The Spanish Franciscan Marcos de Niza made contact with native inhabitants, probably the Sobaipuri, in 1539, and the expedition of Coronado passed through in 1540–1542. The Jesuit Father Kino founded a chain of missions in the Pimería Alta in the 1690s, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> established presidios at Tubac in 1752 and Tucson in 1775.</p><p>The name probably derives from Arizonac, a Spanish rendering of the O'odham alĭ ṣonak, 'small spring', first applied to an area near a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> mining camp in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sonora">Sonora</a>; another suggested origin is the Basque haritz ona, 'the good oak'. After Mexican independence in 1821 the region became part of Alta California. Mexico ceded much of it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1848 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-american-war">Mexican–American War</a>, and the southern strip came with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gadsden-purchase">Gadsden Purchase</a> of 1853. Arizona was admitted on 14 February 1912 as the 48th state and the last of the contiguous states.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Phoenix is the capital and largest city; the original <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitoline-hill">Capitol</a>, with its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> dome, was dedicated in 1901 and is now a museum. The 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 7,151,502 people, most of them living in the Phoenix and Tucson metropolitan areas. About a quarter of the state is Indian reservation land, home to 27 federally recognized tribes, including the Navajo Nation. Grand Canyon National Park is among four national parks in the state, which also has three public universities.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Arizona” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…the Uto-Aztecan family, which stretches from the Great Basin of the western United States, where Shoshone and Comanche were spoken, through the Hopi of <b>Arizona</b> and the Yaqui and Huichol of northern and western Mexico, to its southernmost branch, the Nahuan languages of central Mexico and Central America.</blockquote><a href="/language/nahuatl">Nahuatl · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Many Ancestral Puebloan buildings can be dated to the year their roof beams were felled: in 1929 a charred beam found at Show Low, <b>Arizona</b>, closed the gap in A. E. Douglass’s tree-ring record and gave the ruins exact dates.</blockquote><a href="/culture/ancestral-puebloans">Ancestral Puebloans · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>When Bing Crosby's recording with Lennie Hayton and his orchestra on 27 September 1933 made the song widely popular, its origins were obscure, and in 1934 William and Mary Goodwin sued for $500,000, claiming it copied their &quot;An <b>Arizona</b> Home&quot; of 1905.</blockquote><a href="/songs/home-on-the-range">Home on the Range</a></li><li><blockquote>That bridge was in turn replaced in 1972 and rebuilt at Lake Havasu City, <b>Arizona</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/london-bridge-is-falling-down">London Bridge Is Falling Down</a></li><li><blockquote>One story is that he wrote it in 1940 in warm La Quinta, California, while staying at the La Quinta Hotel, a retreat favoured by Hollywood; the <b>Arizona</b> Biltmore also claims the song was written there.</blockquote><a href="/songs/white-christmas">White Christmas</a></li><li><blockquote>It fixed the modern border of southern <b>Arizona</b> and New Mexico.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-30">30 December in history</a></li></ul></section>
